{"qid":"Q736839","names":["Kamakura Shogunate"],"title":"Kamakura Shogunate","endonym":{"native":"鎌倉幕府","lang":"ja"},"capsule":"Japan's first warrior government, the Kamakura shogunate was established by Minamoto no Yoritomo after his clan's victory in the Genpei War (1180–85); the imperial court in Kyoto continued as a ceremonial institution while real power resided in Kamakura. The Hōjō regents effectively controlled the shogunate from 1199 onward, repelling two Mongol invasion attempts in 1274 and 1281 with the aid of typhoons the Japanese called kamikaze. The regime collapsed in 1333 when Emperor Go-Daigo's restoration movement drew the defection of the general Ashikaga Takauji, who sacked Kamakura and exterminated the Hōjō clan.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The shogunate's core domain in eastern Honshu is well documented; authority over distant provinces fluctuated and was exercised through appointed constables (shugo) whose actual control varied.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Jeffrey P. Mass, 'Warrior Government in Early Medieval Japan' (Yale UP)","url":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctt32bwmh"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Kamakura shogunate","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kamakura_shogunate"}],"britannica":"topic/Kamakura-shogunate","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Kamakura shogunate","rulers":[{"qid":"Q431052","name":"Minamoto no Yoriie","start":1202,"end":1203,"image":"Minamoto no Yoriie.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Minamoto%20no%20Yoriie.jpg?width=320","imageLicense":{"license":"Public domain","artist":"Usiwakamaru","attributionRequired":false},"nat